Longing and grief. And shame. So much shame.

I wrapped the arms of my avatar around my body and thought as loudly as I could of comfort. The warmth of a hearth while sitting under a blanket. The safety a mother provides her child. Or a friend that is always there when needed, and never casts judgement.

"Hey, it's okay. It's not your fault. You'll be okay."

The hug was awkward, an artifice that was neither warmth nor pressure, yet none of it mattered. Sobs wracked my-- Gwion's?-- body. What little composure I'd gathered blinking away tears earlier was all but lost. I was at the edge of a precipice I could barely see the shape of, feeling like freefall was eminent and unavoidable.

And that damning, overwhelming shame kept egging me ever forward.

"Let's go somewhere quieter, alright?" I said, trying to balm the immediate feelings with the promise of safe release.

It was like a switch. All emotions flatlined at once. The desire to cry vanished, and my body stood to walk some place I wasn't privy of. Only the emptiness remained, growing with every second the denial remained in place.

In the minute it took to cut away from noise and gazes, I pondered on my memories. I could feel the shape of them, but they felt distant, like my body. I had an odd certainty I could look and remember, and yet just as curiously I felt like I didn't want to. There was a thin undercurrent of dread that lead me to believe that whatever pretense stood between Gwion and I would crumble the moment I pushed, so I refrained.

When I was sure I wouldn't be interrupted, tucked hugging my knees on the backside of a fence, the feelings came back all at once. Tears flowed freely, yet not a sound escaped my lips, yet. The expected catharsis was spoiled by restraint and needless worries. A flash of a forgotten handkerchief, as well as the ignoble belief that everyone would see the stains lining my cheeks, would know of my failure lest I kept a tight leash on my own body.

"It's alright, let it out," I tried, to some success. "Nobody will notice. Nobody will care, so take the time you need."

